WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 8 Student Video
Unit 2, Lesson 4: Naturalism

1
00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:06.730
A generation ago, in one of the most popular shows on TV, Cosmos, the host

2
00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:12.630
Carl Sagan began each episode by saying that Cosmos is all that is, or was, or

3
00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:13.730
ever will be.

4
00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:30.310
In a nutshell, Carl Sagan was describing the worldview known as naturalism, which is

5
00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:35.310
the view that all that exists is the physical, material world, and in which there is

6
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.090
no supernatural realm.

7
00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:42.930
Naturalism is clearly the predominant worldview in Western culture, shaping

8
00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:47.730
institutions like the educational system, the university, and even the media.

9
00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:55.860
Naturalism offers non-supernatural answers to our four big worldview questions.

10
00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:01.060
First, what is real? According to naturalism, the only things that are real are

11
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.720
those that can be tested by the five senses.

12
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.600
In other words, only those things that have height, weight, extension in space,

13
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.980
and other physical properties.

14
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.960
So essentially, if something can't be weighed on a scale, or seen through a

15
00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.620
microscope, it doesn't exist. So things like God, values, purposes,

16
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.120
objective morality, demons, and the soul are not real.

17
00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.960
But of course, things like grass, water, and sand are real.

18
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.820
So, what is man?

19
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.900
On naturalism, man is not the purposeful creation intended by a loving God

20
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.560
meant to be in relationship with God and with other people.

21
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.320
In fact, the late atheist Victor Stenger captured the naturalistic view of man well.

22
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:58.200
Darwinism implies that humanity developed by accident, contradicting the traditional

23
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.940
teaching that humans are special, created in God's image.

24
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:07.660
So essentially, that means that you and me and all of creation is a cosmic accident.

25
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.620
Now the third question is, what happens at death?

26
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.740
There are really three options of what happens to you when you die.

27
00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:20.500
One would be to face some kind of judgment and go to either heaven or hell.

28
00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.140
The other possibility is to go through some kind of cycle of reincarnation.

29
00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.720
And the last possibility is extinction.

30
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.200
That when you die, you cease to exist.

31
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.400
Now on the naturalistic worldview, since there's no God and there's no soul, when

32
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.200
you stop breathing your last breath, your life is done.

33
00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:46.360
Death is final. In the 1970s, the band Kansas had a

34
00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:51.560
famous song in which they described human beings as merely dust in the wind.

35
00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:55.420
That all our dreams are a mere curiosity.

36
00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.420
So we're like a drop of water in an endless sea.

37
00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:05.220
All that lasts forever is the earth below and the sky above.

38
00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:12.100
So then our fourth question is, what is the basis for right and wrong?

39
00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:19.770
Since naturalists don't believe in God, there's no standard outside of mankind that

40
00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:24.270
determines right and wrong. Well there are a variety of different attempts to

41
00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.770
explain morality from the naturalistic worldview.

42
00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:33.250
Essentially, it boils down to what benefits mankind and helps propagate the species.

43
00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:41.060
Naturalism is nothing new. In fact, centuries before the time of Christ, there were

44
00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:45.540
some Greek philosophers who believed that all reality could be described as these

45
00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:51.080
small, tiny atoms. While naturalism may not be new, it is in direct

46
00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.280
contrast and conflict with the Christian worldview.

47
00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:00.540
The question is, can it capture reality, both as we experience it and know it to

48
00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:00.720
be?

